0045 — dashes on a `<Path>` stroke: SVG's stroke-dasharray and
stroke-dashoffset, as a periodic trim over the cut-point code 0042's trim
already has. The design is `patches/0045-path-primitives.design.md` in this
repository, the numbers `baselines/path-primitives.md`.

What the patch adds, by file:

- `contracts/spec/spec.ts` and the generated `spec.rs`, microts `spec.rs`
  and `component-types-microts.ts`: props `dashCount` (187, int 0..4),
  `dash0`..`dash3` (188-191, f32 lengths in the path's units) and
  `dashOffset` (192, f32) — six of the eleven ids 187-197 that the path
  block left free; anim bit 65 for `dashOffset`; `PATH_DASH_MAX = 4`. No new
  op, no table, no header field: a pattern is style, not geometry.
- `engine/core/src/style.rs`: the six fields on `Resolved`, set and read.
- `engine/core/src/path.rs`: `Dash` and `dash()`, which `emit_stroke` now
  calls in place of `trim()`. A solid pattern — no lengths, a negative or
  non-finite one, more than four, a period not above 0 — is `trim()` itself,
  so every stroke without a pattern is the bytes it was. Otherwise, per
  subpath: the trim window as arc-length spans (each/whole, wrapping, as
  0042 places them), the dashes the pattern places (an odd count repeated
  once, as SVG; the offset the distance into the pattern at the subpath's
  start; period k's dashes start at `k * period + c - phase`, computed from k
  rather than accumulated, so a long outline's dashes do not drift; a dash
  after a zero-length gap continues the one before it), and their
  intersection. So the pattern restarts on every subpath, as SVG's does, and
  a trim reveals or hides dashes without moving them. On a closed subpath a
  dash on at both its end and its start is one piece through the start (a
  join, not two caps), as trim's wrapped window is, and a dash over the
  whole of a subpath keeps it as it was (a closed one keeps its joins all
  the way round). A zero-length dash is a dot: its two caps facing along the
  outline, wound as an open polyline's outline is so it joins the stroke's
  nonzero union — a disc under a round cap, a square under a square cap,
  nothing under a butt cap (SVG's rule for a zero-length subpath). At most
  `DASH_MAX_PIECES` (65,536) dashes a stroke a frame, counting every dash
  the pattern places: a pattern far finer than its outline stops there
  rather than costing the frame without bound. + - * / and comparisons
  only, like the rest of the file.
- `engine/core/src/draw.rs`: the walk passes the node's pattern.
- `engine/core/src/path_scenes.rs`: `PathPaint` carries `dash: Dash::NONE`
  in the three scene constructors, so every pinned scene digest is
  unchanged.
- `framework/compiler/tailwind.ts`: `stroke-dash-[a_b...]` (1 to 4 lengths,
  `_` or `,` between them, each >= 0 and not all 0), `stroke-dash-none` and
  `stroke-dash-offset-[N]` (any sign). A pattern the core would draw solid
  does not parse, which a class= attribute refuses (0041).
- `framework/compiler/animation.ts`: keyframe names `dashOffset`,
  `strokeDashoffset` and `stroke-dashoffset`, as px values. 0043's comment
  that `stroke-dashoffset` is not a spelling is replaced: it is now SVG's
  offset, a length, and trim stays the fraction.

